---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base title="Umi-Time Licensing">
  <div class="umi-region umi-pad">
    <main class="about">
      <section class="hero">
        <h1>Licensing</h1>
        <p>
          Umi‑Time uses a dual‑license model designed to keep the standard open,
          citable, and globally accessible, while ensuring that reference
          implementations remain easy to adopt in any environment — commercial,
          open‑source, or academic.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Umi‑Time Standard (Specification)</h2>
        <p>
          The formal Umi‑Time Standard is published under the
          <strong
            >Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC‑BY‑4.0)</strong
          >
          license. This license allows copying, redistribution, modification, and
          commercial use, provided that appropriate attribution is given.
        </p>
        <p>CC‑BY‑4.0 applies to:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>the canonical HTML specification</li>
          <li>definitions, diagrams, and explanatory text</li>
          <li>mathematical formulas and reference tables</li>
          <li>examples and conceptual descriptions</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This ensures that the Umi‑Time Standard remains open,
          citable,portable, and freely usable in research, education, and
          implementation work.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Reference Implementations</h2>
        <p>
          All official Umi‑Time libraries — including
          <strong>umi‑time‑js</strong> and future language‑specific implementations
          — are licensed under the
          <strong>MIT License</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>The MIT License permits:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>use in commercial and closed‑source software</li>
          <li>modification and redistribution</li>
          <li>sublicensing and integration into larger systems</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This maximizes adoption and ensures that Umi‑Time can be embedded
          anywhere without licensing friction.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Website & Documentation</h2>
        <p>
          The Umi‑Time website and documentation (the <strong>www</strong>
          repository) are licensed under the <strong>MIT License</strong>,
          except where noted.
        </p>
        <p>In particular:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>documentation text is MIT</li>
          <li>code samples are MIT</li>
          <li>
            embedded excerpts from the specification remain under CC‑BY‑4.0,
            since they originate from the standard itself
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This structure keeps the website easy to reuse, reference, and extend,
          while preserving the correct license for spec‑derived content.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Summary</h2>
        <p>Umi‑Time uses a clear dual‑license model:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>CC‑BY‑4.0</strong> for the Umi‑Time Standard</li>
          <li>
            <strong>MIT</strong> for reference implementations and the website
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This combination ensures that the standard remains open and citable,
          while implementations remain maximally usable across all environments.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </div>
</Base>
